# Louvo catalog API — copy to server/.env and fill in.
#
# The API server itself only needs DATABASE_URL. Everything else here is for the
# publish script, which runs on a laptop rather than in the deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Railway injects this into the service automatically.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use the *internal* host for the deployed service (no egress, no TLS) and the
# public proxy host from a laptop.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@localhost:5432/luvo

# auto (default) | on | off — see server/src/db.ts. `auto` means TLS without
# certificate verification for anything that is not a *.railway.internal host.
# DATABASE_SSL=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT is injected by Railway; 8080 is only for a local run.
# PORT=8080
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# CORS_ORIGIN=*
# How long a built catalog is reused before the revision is re-checked.
# CATALOG_CACHE_MS=30000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 — publish only. The server never touches the bucket.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Cloudflare dashboard: R2 -> Overview, the "Account ID" in the sidebar.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Either spelling is accepted; Cloudflare's dashboard says "Bucket name".
R2_BUCKET=luvo-catalogue

# R2 -> Manage API tokens. Needs Object Read & Write on this bucket only.
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Where phones read the images from — a custom domain on the bucket, or its
# r2.dev subdomain. NOT the S3 endpoint above: that one requires credentials,
# and this URL is baked into the catalog every phone downloads.
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.louvo.app

# Any S3-compatible endpoint works — the publish script signs SigV4 and nothing
# in it is Cloudflare-specific. Set this to move to B2, Bunny or S3.
# R2_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# R2_REGION=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publish source
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset, the publish script reads src/api/mockCatalog.ts — the authoring format.
# Set it to publish from a live catalog instead.
# CATALOG_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Preview generation — the API mints the signed urls, the worker does the work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both are optional. Without them the service is exactly what it was: a catalog
# API. /v1/previews answers 503 previews_unconfigured and the app falls back the
# same way it falls back to the bundled catalog.

# A SECOND bucket, private, with no public domain and no CDN in front of it.
# Never the catalog bucket: that one is public and cached immutable for a year,
# which is precisely what a photograph of somebody's face must never be.
# The same R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID / R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY / R2_ACCOUNT_ID above are
# reused, so the token needs Object Read & Write on both buckets.
PREVIEW_BUCKET=luvo-transient

# The generator key. Server-side only — this is the whole point of phase 2, and
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FAL_KEY should be removed from app builds once this is live.
FAL_KEY=

# The try-on model. Deliberately not the generators' FAL_EDIT_MODEL: one switch
# moving both would re-point a generator whose model change means re-shooting
# 103 renders. See src/api/tryOn.ts for what each of these costs.
# TRY_ON_MODEL=openai/gpt-image-2/edit
# TRY_ON_QUALITY=medium
# TRY_ON_IMAGE_SIZE=match
# TRY_ON_RESOLUTION=2K

# fal's concurrency limit for the account, which fal sets from credits purchased
# in the last four weeks: 10 at $10, 20 at $100, 30 at $500, 40 at $1000+.
# This is not a safety margin — submitting past the real limit buys rejections,
# not throughput. Raise it when the plan is raised, and not before.
# FAL_MAX_INFLIGHT=10
# How many of those slots one device may hold. One, until the limit is much
# larger: with ten in total, a user pressing Generate five times owns half the
# queue and everybody else waits behind them.
# FAL_MAX_INFLIGHT_PER_DEVICE=1

# How long the phone has to upload before its job is abandoned, and how long a
# signed download url lasts. Minutes, not hours, for both.
# PREVIEW_UPLOAD_TTL_S=900
# PREVIEW_DOWNLOAD_TTL_S=900

# The hand-off window. NOT a retention policy: a preview lives on the phone that
# generated it, permanently, until its owner deletes it. This is how long our
# copy waits to be collected before it is deleted unread.
# PREVIEW_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# Refused above this, before a signature is minted. The app downscales to a
# ~1536px longest edge first, so a real upload is a few hundred KB.
# PREVIEW_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=12582912

# How often the worker looks for something to do.
# WORKER_POLL_MS=2000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sharing and referral — all optional, and all of it degrades to something
# honest. See docs/sharing.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public origin serving /s/<code>. Unset, the landing page uses the origin
# the request arrived on, which on Railway is the deployment url and works.
# Set it once a real domain points here, so links in other people's chat
# histories keep resolving after a redeploy.
# SHARE_BASE_URL=https://louvo.app

# Must agree with `expo.scheme` in app.json. This is what makes a shared link
# open the app somebody already has rather than a page telling them to install.
# APP_SCHEME=luvo

# Where the landing page sends someone who does not have the app. With neither
# set the page offers the deep link alone rather than linking somewhere that
# 404s — a broken download button is a worse advertisement than an absent one.
# IOS_APP_STORE_URL=https://apps.apple.com/app/id0000000000
# ANDROID_PLAY_URL=https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.luvoai.luvo

# ANDROID_PACKAGE=com.luvoai.luvo
# IOS_BUNDLE_ID=com.luvoai.luvo
# The numeric App Store id, for the iOS smart app banner.
# IOS_APP_ID=0000000000

# Universal / app links. Both association files 404 until these are set, on
# purpose: an apple-app-site-association naming a team id that is not ours is
# worse than none, because iOS caches it.
# IOS_TEAM_ID=ABCDE12345
# ANDROID_SHA256_FINGERPRINTS=AA:BB:CC:...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credits and accounts — see docs/credits.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ON by default, and the one value here worth a decision before deploying. On,
# a device gets FREE_GENERATIONS previews and then needs an account and a
# purchase. Off, generation behaves exactly as it did before credits existed.
#
# Off is for a local checkout, where two generations is not enough to iterate on
# a prompt. It is not a production setting: a deployment with this off is giving
# previews away at about five cents each.
# CREDITS_ENFORCED=true
# FREE_GENERATIONS=2

# The salt under which install anchors and email codes are hashed.
#
# ROTATING THIS RESETS EVERY FREE ALLOWANCE — every anchor hashes to an id that
# has never been seen, so every device is owed two again. Set it once, keep it.
# ANCHOR_SALT=

# --- Sign in ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Every provider is optional and each refuses rather than guesses: with no Apple
# audience configured, an Apple sign-in answers `apple_unconfigured` instead of
# accepting a token it cannot verify.

# The app's bundle id, and the Services ID too if web sign-in is ever added.
# Defaults to IOS_BUNDLE_ID.
# APPLE_AUDIENCES=com.luvoai.luvo

# EVERY OAuth client id that can mint a token for this app. iOS, Android and Web
# are three different ids and the token's `aud` is whichever one the phone used,
# so all three belong here, comma-separated.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=1234-web.apps.googleusercontent.com,1234-ios.apps.googleusercontent.com

# Email sign-in. With no RESEND_API_KEY the endpoint answers 503 rather than
# pretending to have sent something.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Louvo <hello@louvo.app>
# EMAIL_CODE_TTL_S=600
# EMAIL_CODE_ATTEMPTS=5

# Returns the sign-in code in the API response instead of mailing it.
#
# A local-development affordance and a genuine hole — anyone who can call the
# endpoint can sign in as any address. It is ignored outright whenever
# RESEND_API_KEY is set, because the only deployment with both meant to turn
# this off.
# EMAIL_DEV_ECHO=false

# --- RevenueCat ------------------------------------------------------------
# The shared secret. Paste the same string into the Authorization field of the
# webhook in the RevenueCat dashboard.
#
# Without it the webhook refuses every delivery, which is correct: an open
# endpoint that grants credits grants them to whoever finds it. It is also the
# ONLY thing in this service that adds a credit — the app never does.
# REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Stripe ----------------------------------------------------------------
# How the WEB is paid. The app keeps StoreKit and Play through RevenueCat.
#
# Both of these are secret and both live only here. There is deliberately no
# publishable key anywhere in this repository: checkout is HOSTED, so the visitor
# leaves for Stripe's own page and no card field, no Stripe.js and no client key
# ever exist in the browser bundle.
#
# Dashboard → Developers → API keys. Use the test key (sk_test_…) until you mean
# it; test and live are separate accounts with separate Price ids, which is why
# the Price a pack points at is a database row rather than a constant.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...

# Dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint → Signing secret.
#
# Point the endpoint at  https://<this-api>/v1/webhooks/stripe  and subscribe to
#   checkout.session.completed
#   checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded    (delayed payment methods)
#   charge.refunded                             (the money going back out)
#
# WITHOUT THIS THE WEBHOOK REFUSES EVERY DELIVERY, which is correct: an endpoint
# that grants credits with no signature check grants them to whoever finds it.
# Locally, `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/v1/webhooks/stripe` prints
# a whsec_ of its own — use that one.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Where a finished checkout sends the browser back to: the WEBSITE's origin, not
# this API's. The browser sends a path and this is the origin it is resolved
# against, which is what makes an open redirect impossible rather than unlikely.
# Falls back to SHARE_BASE_URL, and then to the request's own Origin header.
# CHECKOUT_RETURN_URL=https://louvo.app

# Optional. Stripe Tax, if it is enabled on the account.
# STRIPE_AUTOMATIC_TAX=false

# Whether Checkout issues a real invoice per payment. On by default, and it is
# what puts a downloadable PDF behind the Invoice button on /account — Stripe is
# the only party here with a number sequence, a tax registration and an address
# to put on one. Turning it off changes nothing about the purchase or the
# credits; the button falls back to Stripe's hosted receipt for the charge.
# Not retroactive: a payment taken before this was on has a receipt and never an
# invoice, and the account page says which of the two it opened.
# STRIPE_INVOICES=true

# Pinned rather than left to the dashboard default, so a response shape cannot
# move under a running deployment. Only change this deliberately.
# STRIPE_API_VERSION=2024-06-20

# A TEST HOOK, and the only one in this service. `npm run sandbox` points it at
# its own process so a whole purchase can be walked with no key and no network.
# Never set it on a real deployment.
# STRIPE_API_BASE=https://api.stripe.com

# Once per Stripe account, after the key above is set:
#   npm run stripe:setup            create a Product and Price per pack
#   npm run stripe:setup -- --list  what is pointed where
# It writes credit_products.stripe_price_id. There is no price column and there
# never will be — the Price object is the authority, and this is a pointer to it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in on the web, and the anonymous tier in front of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clerk is how the *website* signs in; the app keeps Apple and Google. Only the
# issuer is needed: a Clerk session token is a JWT verified against the
# published JWKS, so this server holds no Clerk credential at all. The secret
# key belongs to the Next.js app and a copy here would be held for nothing.
#
# The Frontend API origin. Derived from the publishable key if you need to
# find it: base64-decode everything after the pk_test_/pk_live_ prefix.
CLERK_ISSUER=https://crisp-dog-7998.clerk.accounts.dev
# Optional: pin the origins tokens may be minted for. Empty means do not check,
# which is safe because the issuer and the signature already establish the
# token came from our Clerk instance.
# CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES=https://louvo.app,http://localhost:3000

# The free allowance, which is smaller in a browser than on a phone because
# localStorage is not a keystore. See server/src/abuse.ts.
# FREE_GENERATIONS=2
# WEB_FREE_GENERATIONS=1
# Credits granted once, the first time an account signs in. One, so somebody
# who has not spent their anonymous preview has two in total afterwards.
# SIGNUP_BONUS_CREDITS=1

# The anonymous ceiling: what stops clearing localStorage from being a way to
# mint a new allowance. A bucket is one client IP plus a coarse browser/OS
# class, so several people in one house are several buckets.
# ANON_MAX_PER_BUCKET=3
# ANON_WINDOW_S=86400
# Distinct device secrets from one bucket before an email is sent. Above the
# generation cap on purpose: hitting the cap is three flatmates, minting five
# identities is a loop. Needs RESEND_API_KEY to actually send.
# ANON_ALERT_DEVICES=5
# ABUSE_ALERT_EMAIL=you@example.com
